h_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which number is a zero of $f(x) = x^2-1$?","options":["$1$","$0$","$2$","$\\frac12$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e6ae1999ba69b711ff11ea72"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which statement about $f(x) = 2^x$ matches the definition?","options":["$f$ has no zeros.","$x = 0$ is a zero.","$x = 1$ is a zero.","$x = -1$ is a zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a10520db0ae7f03f7cc130db"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Graphically, where does a zero of a function occur?","options":["Where the graph crosses the $y$-axis.","Where the graph has a vertical asymptote.","Where the output is largest.","Where the graph crosses or touches the $x$-axis."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f13ada60cc9e9cb8d30da31a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which term is also used for a zero of a function?","options":["Upper bound","Root","Union","Vertical asymptote"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"662986ac36ea0d3c9e493879"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = x-4$, which input is a zero?","options":["$0$","$-4$","$4$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"116cc5e1a9c0e2d827431abc"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = (x-2)(x+3)$, which value is a zero?","options":["$3$","$2$","$-2$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1dd4476b1ec9a4661eb68d2d"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly handles the phrase 'crosses or touches the $x$-axis'?","options":["A zero occurs only when the graph crosses the $x$-axis.","A zero may occur even when the graph only touches the $x$-axis.","A zero occurs only at the $y$-axis.","A zero cannot be visible from the graph."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8f5699e567a7e75b10be0a82"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement avoids confusing the input with the output?","options":["A zero is always the output value $0$, not an input.","A zero is any input value equal to $0$.","A zero is any point where $f(x)$ is undefined.","A zero is an input value whose output is $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ffdd364dee51ec389dbaea0a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(2) = 0$ and $f(5) = 3$. Which conclusion is correct?","options":["$2$ is a zero of $f$.","$5$ is a zero of $f$.","$3$ is a zero of $f$.","$0$ is not related to any zero of $f$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b9e15d79fd0b6c70fa678ebb"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_zero_of_a_function_3885eade_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = x^2$, which statement about zeros is correct?","options":["$1$ is a zero because $f(1) = 1$.","$0$ is a zero because $f(0) = 0$.","$-1$ is a zero because $f(-1) = 1$.","Every real number is a zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c5d438b25c4e6ac0c8398620"},{"id":"zero_of_a_function_new_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A zero of $f$ is an input that satisfies which equation?","options":["$x=0$","$f(x)=0$","$f(x)=1$","$f'(x)=0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"726b2d67c0c7535839d52f20"},{"id":"zero_of_a_function_new_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which term can also mean a zero of a function?","options":["Root.","Upper bound.","Asymptote.","Codomain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0ea30cb83affd23266fee970"},{"id":"zero_of_a_function_new_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Where is a zero seen on the graph of a function?","options":["Where the graph crosses the $y$-axis only.","Where the graph has a vertical asymptote.","Where the function is undefined.","Where the graph crosses or touches the $x$-axis."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fc7b5c4a0d7f76e51fe7238b"},{"id":"zero_of_a_function_new_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which value is a zero of $f(x)=x(x-2)$?","options":["$1$","$3$","$2$","$-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"789f91933977ff1bc26fc878"},{"id":"zero_of_a_function_new_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function has no real zeros?","options":["$f(x)=x-1$","$f(x)=x^2-1$","$f(x)=x^2+1$","$f(x)=x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4b29dcf3c8b0547b69c4c148"},{"id":"zero_of_a_function_new_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(0)=0$, what can be concluded?","options":["$f$ has no zeros.","$0$ is a vertical asymptote.","$f$ is constant.","$0$ is a zero of $f$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a8b3db9863a460fa3b2b6808"},{"id":"zero_of_a_function_new_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is a zero an input value rather than an output value?","options":["It is always the value of $f(x)$ when $x=0$.","It is the largest value in the range.","It is the derivative at a stationary point, universally and without exception.","It is the value of $x$ that makes the output $f(x)$ equal to $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e2b1660dc7166f1c31cb5814"},{"id":"zero_of_a_function_new_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x)=(x-1)^2$, what happens at $x=1$?","options":["$x=1$ is not a zero because the graph does not cross.","$x=1$ is a vertical asymptote.","$x=1$ is a zero where the graph touches the $x$-axis.","$x=1$ is outside the domain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6589721a3aa5438edf0921c7"}]}}